$$ Interrogate a live deadlock, non-invasively.
$$
$$ A hung process is better evidence than a dead one: every thread is still
$$ standing where it stopped. Read-only, and it detaches rather than quitting,
$$ so the game survives and can be dumped again if the first look is not enough.

.sympath srv*C:\Users\zigmaster\.cursor\projects\f-rbo-fabre-proto-d3d9-sw\..\symbols-cache*https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols;F:\rbo_fabre_proto\d3d9_sw\symbols
.reload /f

.echo ===MODULES===
lm m d3d11
lm m mono*
lm m ntdll

$$ The theory being tested: our own record spinlock was frozen in the held state
$$ by a restore, so every later JIT registration piles up in fntab_lock, which is
$$ inside d3d11.dll. If that is what happened, many threads share a stack whose
$$ top frame is in d3d11 and whose caller is mono.
.echo ===ALL STACKS===
~*k

.echo ===DUMP===
.dump /ma F:\rbo_fabre_proto\d3d9_sw\hang_live.dmp

.echo ===DONE===
qd
